==Background==
[[File:Adidas UEFA final footballs, June 2024 07.jpg|thumb|right|An example of the [[Adidas]] balls used in the final on display in London in 2024.]]
For Manchester City, this was their third European final and second European Cup/Champions League final appearance; they won the [[1970 European Cup Winners' Cup final]] but lost the [[2021 UEFA Champions League final]] 1–0 to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], who were the most recent new European champions, having won in [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]]. Manager [[Pep Guardiola]] was looking to win his third UEFA Champions League title and the first since [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]] with former employers [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], when they beat [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 3–1 at [[Wembley Stadium]]. He was also aiming to become the first manager to win two European trebles, having done so with Barcelona in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2008–09]]. Additionally, Manchester City were attempting to secure the [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]], having earlier won the [[2022–23 Premier League|Premier League]] and [[2022–23 FA Cup|FA Cup]]. After [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]'s triumph in the [[2023 UEFA Europa Conference League final]] three days earlier, they became the English team with the longest European major trophy drought—53 years since their latest triumph. 

On the other hand, this was Inter Milan's twelfth European final and sixth European Cup/Champions League final appearance, having won 3–1 in [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] against [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], 1–0 against [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] at [[San Siro|home]] in [[1965 European Cup final|1965]], and 2–0 against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]], resulting in Inter completing the continental treble; they also lost 2–1 to [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] in [[1967 European Cup final|1967]] and 2–0 to [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] in [[1972 European Cup final|1972]], enabling those clubs to win the continental treble themselves. The club had also contested five UEFA Cup/UEFA Europa League finals, winning in [[1991 UEFA Cup final|1991]], [[1994 UEFA Cup final|1994]], and [[1998 UEFA Cup final|1998]] as well as losing in [[1997 UEFA Cup final|1997]] and [[2020 UEFA Europa League final|2020]]. They also contested the [[2010 UEFA Super Cup]], losing 2–0 to [[Atlético Madrid]]. Inter was also the most recent Italian club to win the Champions League; since 2010 only one other Italian club—[[Juventus FC|Juventus]]—had reached the Champions League final, losing in [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]] and [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]]. Having won the [[2023 Coppa Italia final]] against [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Onyeagwara |first=Nnamdi |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4550046/2023/05/24/inter-coppa-italia-fiorentina/ |title=Inter win second consecutive Coppa Italia after beating Fiorentina 2–1 in final |work=[[The Athletic]] |date=24 May 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023}}</ref> Inter were attempting to win a [[Double (association football)#European cup double|cup double]]. Manager [[Simone Inzaghi]] was aiming for his first European title as manager and the second in his football career, having won against Manchester United in the [[1999 UEFA Super Cup]] as a [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] player. Their midfielder [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]] was aiming to become the 11th player to have [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of players to have won the three main European club competitions|won all three major European trophies]] and the first to have achieved that with three different clubs, having won the [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]] with Manchester United and the [[2021–22 UEFA Europa Conference League]] with [[AS Roma|Roma]].

This was the first meeting in [[UEFA club competitions|UEFA competitions]] between City and Inter. City had won six matches against [[Italian football clubs in international competitions|Italian clubs]], while Inter had won 16 matches against [[English football clubs in international competitions|English clubs]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=UEFA Press Kits |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/mediaservices/informationkits/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2023/match/2037765/ |access-date=19 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=UEFA |archive-date=19 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230519093646/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/informationkits/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2023/match/2037765 |url-status=live }}</ref>

