===Manchester City===
{{see also|2022–23 Manchester City F.C. season}}
[[File:Erling Haaland 2023 (cropped-v2).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Erling Haaland]], who joined [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] at the beginning of the [[2022–23 Manchester City F.C. season|2022–23 season]] from [[Borussia Dortmund]], was the top scorer in the competition with 12 goals en route to the final.]]

Manchester City qualified for the Champions League group stage by winning the [[2021–22 Premier League]]. In the group stage, they were drawn into Group G, alongside [[2021–22 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] runners-up [[Borussia Dortmund]], [[2021–22 La Liga|La Liga]] fourth-place team [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] and [[2021–22 Danish Superliga|Danish Superliga]] champions [[F.C. Copenhagen|Copenhagen]].<ref name="UEFA">{{cite web |title=UEFA Champions League group stage draw |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/draws/2023/2001673/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=8 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220608120149/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/draws/2023/2001673/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In City's opener of the group stage, they faced Sevilla at the [[Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium|Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán]] and won 4–0, with goals from [[Phil Foden]], [[Rúben Dias]] and a brace from [[Erling Haaland]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Sevilla-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035654--sevilla-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516211105/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035654--sevilla-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 2, City won 2–1 at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] against Borussia Dortmund, with goals from [[John Stones]] and Erling Haaland.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Dortmund |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035669--man-city-vs-dortmund/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516204044/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035669--man-city-vs-dortmund/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 3, City defeated Copenhagen at home 5–0, with goals from [[Riyad Mahrez]], [[Julián Álvarez]], a brace from Erling Haaland and an own goal from [[Davit Khocholava]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Copenhagen |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035685--man-city-vs-copenhagen/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516204707/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035685--man-city-vs-copenhagen/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 4, City drew in Copenhagen 0–0.<ref>{{cite web |title=Copenhagen-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035701--copenhagen-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516204047/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035701--copenhagen-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 5, City drew against Borussia Dortmund at the [[Westfalenstadion]] 0–0.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dortmund-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035718--dortmund-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516204725/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035718--dortmund-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 6, at the City of Manchester Stadium, the hosts defeated Sevilla 3–1, with goals from [[Rico Lewis]], Julian Álvarez and Riyad Mahrez.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Sevilla |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035733--man-city-vs-sevilla/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516204105/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035733--man-city-vs-sevilla/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the round of 16, City were drawn against German club [[RB Leipzig]]. In the first leg held at the [[Red Bull Arena (Leipzig)|Red Bull Arena]], City drew 1–1, with goals from Riyad Mahrez and [[Joško Gvardiol]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Leipzig-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036588--leipzig-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=18 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230518135930/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036588--leipzig-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the reverse leg, City defeated Leipzig 7–0, with five goals from Erling Haaland, tying a Champions League single game goals record, and one goal each from [[İlkay Gündoğan]] and [[Kevin De Bruyne]], to win 8–1 on aggregate and advance to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Leipzig |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036594--man-city-vs-leipzig/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=22 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230322214332/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036594--man-city-vs-leipzig/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, City were drawn against German champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]. In the first leg, at the City of Manchester Stadium, the hosts won 3–0, with goals from [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]], [[Bernardo Silva]] and Erling Haaland.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Bayern |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037770--man-city-vs-bayern/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516215649/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037770--man-city-vs-bayern/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the second leg, at the [[Allianz Arena]], City drew 1–1, with goals from Erling Haaland and [[Joshua Kimmich]], winning 4–1 on aggregate and qualifying for the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bayern-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037772--bayern-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=27 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230427062434/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037772--bayern-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the semi-finals, City were drawn against Spanish champions and [[2021–22 UEFA Champions League|reigning UEFA Champions League winners]] [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], making it a rematch of the last season's tie at the same stage, which Madrid won 6–5 on aggregate after extra time. In the first leg, at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium|Santiago Bernabéu]], City and Real drew, with a first-half goal by [[Vinícius Júnior]] matched by a Kevin De Bruyne equaliser.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid-Man City |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037797--real-madrid-vs-man-city/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517005332/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037797--real-madrid-vs-man-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the second leg, at the City of Manchester Stadium, the hosts won 4–0, with a brace from Bernardo Silva and goals by [[Manuel Akanji]] and Julián Álvarez. City won 5–1 on aggregate to qualify for their second Champions League final in three years.<ref>{{cite web |title=Man City-Real Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037800--man-city-vs-real-madrid/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517005333/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037800--man-city-vs-real-madrid/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Inter Milan===
{{see also|2022–23 Inter Milan season}}

Inter Milan qualified for the Champions League group stage by finishing as runners-up in the [[2021–22 Serie A]]. In the group stage, they were drawn into Group C alongside [[2021–22 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], [[2021–22 La Liga|La Liga]] runners-up [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] and [[2021–22 Czech First League|Czech First League]] champions [[FC Viktoria Plzeň|Viktoria Plzeň]].<ref name="UEFA"/>

In the opening match of the group stage, Inter faced Bayern Munich at the [[San Siro]] and lost 0–2, with goals for Bayern from [[Leroy Sané]] and an own goal by [[Danilo D'Ambrosio]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Inter-Bayern |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035646--inter-vs-bayern/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516211650/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035646--inter-vs-bayern/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 2, Inter won 2–0 against Viktoria Plzeň at the [[Doosan Arena]], with goals from [[Edin Džeko]] and [[Denzel Dumfries]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Plzeň-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035661--plzen-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=8 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230408025528/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035661--plzen-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 3, Inter returned to the San Siro and won 1–0 against Barcelona, with the only goal of the match coming from [[Hakan Çalhanoğlu]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Inter-Barcelona |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035678--inter-vs-barcelona/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516215711/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035678--inter-vs-barcelona/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 4, Inter drew 3–3 with Barcelona at the [[Camp Nou]], with [[Nicolò Barella]], [[Lautaro Martínez]] and [[Robin Gosens]] scoring for Inter, and [[Robert Lewandowski]] (twice) and [[Ousmane Dembélé]] scoring for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web |title=Barcelona-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035693--barcelona-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=14 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230314214627/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035693--barcelona-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 5, Inter won 4–0 against Viktoria Plzeň at the San Siro, with goals from [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]], Džeko and [[Romelu Lukaku]], sealing qualification to the knockout phase.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |title=Inter-Plzeň |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035709--inter-vs-plzen/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=14 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230314214627/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035709--inter-vs-plzen/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On matchday 6, Inter lost 2–0 to Bayern Munich at the [[Allianz Arena]], with goals from [[Benjamin Pavard]] and [[Eric Maxim Choupo-Moting]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Bayern-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035725--bayern-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=14 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230314214627/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2035725--bayern-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the round of 16, Inter were drawn against Portuguese club [[FC Porto|Porto]]. In the first leg held at the San Siro, Inter won 1–0, with a goal from Lukaku.<ref>{{cite web |title=Inter-Porto |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036589--inter-vs-porto/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=14 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230314214328/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036589--inter-vs-porto/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the reverse leg at the [[Estádio do Dragão]], Inter drew 0–0 to seal a 1–0 aggregate victory and qualify for the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Porto-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036595--porto-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230316221545/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2036595--porto-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Inter were drawn against another Portuguese club, [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]]. In the first leg at the [[Estádio da Luz]], Inter won 2–0, with goals from Barella and a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] from Lukaku.<ref>{{cite web |title=Benfica-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037769--benfica-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=16 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230516215711/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037769--benfica-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the second leg at the San Siro, Inter drew 3–3, with goals from Barella, Martínez and [[Joaquín Correa]] for Inter, and [[Fredrik Aursnes]], [[António Silva (footballer)|António Silva]] and [[Petar Musa]] for the visitors, winning 5–3 on aggregate and sealing semi-finals qualification.<ref>{{cite web |title=Inter-Benfica |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037775--inter-vs-benfica/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=13 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230513211313/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037775--inter-vs-benfica/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Inter were drawn against [[Derby della Madonnina|local rivals]] [[AC Milan|Milan]], their first meeting in [[UEFA club competitions|European competition]] since [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League knockout stage#Quarter-finals|2005]], with both legs being held at the San Siro. In the 'away' leg, Inter won 2–0, with goals from Džeko and Mkhitaryan.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |title=Milan-Inter |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037798--milan-vs-inter/ |access-date=17 May 2023 |website=UEFA.com|archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517005333/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037798--milan-vs-inter/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the home leg, Inter won 1–0, with the only goal of the match coming from Martínez, qualifying Inter for the final with a 3–0 aggregate victory. It was the club's sixth European Cup final appearance and their first since [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]], when [[José Mourinho]] was manager.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |title=Inter-Milan |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037799--inter-vs-milan/ |access-date=2023-05-18 |website=UEFA.com|language=en |archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517045352/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/2037799--inter-vs-milan/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Pre-match==

===Identity===
The visual identity of the 2023 UEFA Champions League final was unveiled at the group stage draw in Istanbul on 25 August 2022.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0278-15f34aee6aaa-da414088c652-1000/ |title=Brand identity for UEFA Champions League final in Istanbul unveiled |publisher=UEFA |date=25 August 2022 |access-date=10 June 2023}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Szymon Marciniak.JPG|thumb|upright=.7|Polish referee [[Szymon Marciniak]] officiated the final.]]

On 22 May 2023, Polish referee [[Szymon Marciniak]] was appointed to take charge of the final by UEFA, along with fellow Poles: Paweł Sokolnicki and [[Tomasz Listkiewicz]] as [[Assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]] and Tomasz Kwiatkowski as the [[video assistant referee]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/news/uefa-reveal-szymon-marciniak-referee-133600509.html |title=UEFA reveal Szymon Marciniak will referee Champions League final |work=[[Yahoo! Sports]] |date=22 May 2023 |access-date=22 May 2023 |archive-date=22 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230522150612/https://sports.yahoo.com/news/uefa-reveal-szymon-marciniak-referee-133600509.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="officials" /> The four officials had all previously officiated together in the [[2022 FIFA World Cup final|World Cup final]] six months prior.<ref>{{cite web |date=14 December 2022 |title=Poland's Szymon Marciniak to referee FIFA World Cup final |url=https://www.fifa.com/technical/refereeing/news/szymon-marciniak-from-poland-to-referee-fifa-world-cup-final |access-date=14 December 2022 |publisher=[[FIFA]] |archive-date=16 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221216125623/https://www.fifa.com/technical/refereeing/news/szymon-marciniak-from-poland-to-referee-fifa-world-cup-final |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Szymon Marciniak poprowadzi finał Mistrzostw Świata! |url=https://pzpn.pl/federacja/aktualnosci/2022-12-15/szymon-marciniak-poprowadzi-final-mistrzostw-swiata |access-date=15 December 2022 |publisher=[[Polish Football Association]] |language=pl |archive-date=15 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221215174017/https://pzpn.pl/federacja/aktualnosci/2022-12-15/szymon-marciniak-poprowadzi-final-mistrzostw-swiata |url-status=live }}</ref> They were joined by fellow countryman Bartosz Frankowski as assistant VAR, with Romanian [[István Kovács (referee)|István Kovács]] appointed as the fourth official.<ref name="officials" /> Some controversy regarding the appointment was raised in early June due to multiple media reports that Marciniak had been present at an event organised by [[Sławomir Mentzen]], a [[Right-wing politics|right-wing]] Polish politician and leader of the [[Libertarianism|libertarian]] [[New Hope (Poland)|New Hope]] party.<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Stone|first1=Simon|last2=Easton|first2=Adam|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65785202|title=Champions League final referee Szymon Marciniak attended event organised by Polish far-right leader|website=BBC Sport|date=1 June 2023|access-date=2 June 2023|archive-date=2 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602101108/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65785202|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Polish ref cleared of wrongdoing, will take charge of Champions League final |url=https://tvpworld.com/70289061/polish-ref-cleared-of-wrongdoing-will-take-charge-of-champions-league-final |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231216134715/https://tvpworld.com/70289061/polish-ref-cleared-of-wrongdoing-will-take-charge-of-champions-league-final |archive-date=16 December 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023 |work=TVP World |publisher=[[Telewizja Polska]] }}</ref> UEFA investigated the incident and subsequently decided to maintain the original refereeing line-up on 2 June, having acknowledged Marciniak's "profound apologies and clarification".<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA statement concerning Szymon Marciniak |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0282-1828af52b2ad-df8e29dfca7b-1000--uefa-statement-concerning-szymon-marciniak/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=UEFA |date=2 June 2023 |access-date=2 June 2023 |archive-date=2 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602102318/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/news/0282-1828af52b2ad-df8e29dfca7b-1000--uefa-statement-concerning-szymon-marciniak/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the final was former Turkish international [[Hamit Altıntop]], who was originally the ambassador for 2020 and 2021 finals before those matches were relocated to [[Lisbon]] and [[Porto]], respectively.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burke |first1=Chris |last2=Tozar |first2=Türker |date=8 June 2023 |title=Hamit Altıntop on the final and Istanbul |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0282-18346b49c056-6665da36f49b-1000--hamit-altintop-on-the-final-and-istanbul/ |work=[[UEFA]]|publisher=UEFA |access-date=10 June 2023}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
{{multiple image 
| perrow = 3
| total_width = 300
| image1 = Alesso profile.png
| image2 = Untold 2024 -Burna Boy (53927293629) (cropped).jpg
| image3 = COMBATCHY - Espaço das Américas (37663558155).jpg
