==Post-match==
[[File:Josep Guardiola 2023-10-04 Fußball, Männer, UEFA Champions League, RB Leipzig - Manchester City FC 1DX 2797 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|Manchester City's [[Pep Guardiola]] won his third Champions League title as a manager, and fourth overall.]]
With their victory, Manchester City secured their first UEFA Champions League title, the first new winners of the competition since fellow English club Chelsea in 2012. City became the sixth English club, and 23rd overall team, to win the European Cup, which marked the 15th time an English club were champions of the competition.<ref>{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |title=European Champions' Cup/Champions League |work=[[RSSSF]] |date=27 January 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-date=6 July 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090706013641/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/ec1.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Manchester City also completed the tenth [[Treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]] in European football history, doing so as the eighth overall team and second English club after [[Manchester derby|city rivals]] [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in [[1998–99 Manchester United F.C. season|1998–99]].<ref name="Guardian-Guardiola">{{cite news |last=Jackson |first=Jamie |date=10 June 2023 |title='You have to be lucky': Manchester City's Pep Guardiola grateful for small margins |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/jun/11/you-have-to-be-lucky-manchester-citys-pep-guardiola-grateful-for-small-margins |work=The Guardian |accessdate=10 June 2023 |archive-date=11 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230611001118/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2023/jun/11/you-have-to-be-lucky-manchester-citys-pep-guardiola-grateful-for-small-margins |url-status=live }}</ref> It was the 15th occasion a club [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#Unbeaten sides|won the European Cup unbeaten]], with City the 11th club to accomplish the feat.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://en.as.com/en/2022/03/09/soccer/1646818704_796121.html |title=Has any team won every game in a Champions League season? |work=[[Diario AS]] |date=9 March 2022 |access-date=11 June 2023 |archive-date=23 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230323181613/https://en.as.com/en/2022/03/09/soccer/1646818704_796121.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Pep Guardiola]] won his third Champions League final as a manager after his two titles with [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]] and [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]], becoming the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winning managers|second-most successful coach in the competition]], behind [[Carlo Ancelotti]]'s four victories.<ref>{{cite web |last=Di Maggio |first=Roberto |url=https://www.rsssf.org/players/ec-coach.html |title=European Cups – Performances by Coach |work=[[RSSSF]] |date=18 August 2022 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-date=20 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221120215108/https://www.rsssf.org/players/ec-coach.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He also became the first manager to win two European trebles, having also done so with Barcelona in [[2008–09 FC Barcelona season|2008–09]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Banerjee |first=Apratim |url=https://www.sportskeeda.com/football/news-pep-guardiola-makes-history-winning-treble-manchester-city |title=Pep Guardiola makes history after winning treble with Manchester City |work=[[Sportskeeda]] |date=11 June 2023 |access-date=11 June 2023}}</ref> It was Guardiola's twelfth major trophy with Manchester City; after the match, he stated that "it was written in the stars that we'd win this season—and we did."<ref name="Guardian-Guardiola"/> City midfielder [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]], who scored the lone goal of the game, was selected as the [[Player of the match#Association football|man of the match]] by UEFA's technical observers.<ref name="MOTM"/> Backup goalkeeper [[Scott Carson]] was the only City player to have previously won the competition, doing so as a reserve for [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]] at the same stadium. He thus equalled the record for the longest gap between a first and last European Cup title, shared with Milan's [[Paolo Maldini]] and [[Alessandro Costacurta]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Stanley |first=Colman |url=https://www.balls.ie/football/scott-carson-champions-league-558433 |title=Scott Carson On The Verge Of Astonishing Champions League Achievement |work=[[Balls.ie]] |date=10 June 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-date=10 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610181429/https://www.balls.ie/football/scott-carson-champions-league-558433 |url-status=live }}</ref> Forward [[Julián Alvarez]], who won the [[2022 FIFA World Cup]] with [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] six months prior, became the first player to win the treble and [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]] in the same season. This also made him the tenth player to accomplish the European Cup and World Cup double.<ref>{{cite web |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/65859398 |title=Julian Alvarez: Manchester City striker makes history with Champions League win |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=10 June 2023 |access-date=11 June 2023 |archive-date=10 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610231606/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/65859398 |url-status=live }}</ref>

As winners, Manchester City qualified for the [[2023 UEFA Super Cup]], where they defeated Spanish club [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], winners of the [[2022–23 UEFA Europa League]], [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|on penalties]] following a 1–1 draw after regulation time.<ref>{{cite news |last=Brennan |first=Feargal |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/uefa-super-cup-2023-stadium-champions-league-europa-league/zemmmyqs57bsodqzlqk8hn27 |title=Where is UEFA Super Cup 2023? Host stadium, city for match between Champions League and Europa League winners |work=[[The Sporting News]] |date=10 June 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-date=9 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230609081938/https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/uefa-super-cup-2023-stadium-champions-league-europa-league/zemmmyqs57bsodqzlqk8hn27 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=16 August 2023 |title=Man City beat Sevilla to Lift the First European Trophy of the 2023-24 season |url=https://offsidetrap.in/man-city-beat-sevilla-to-lift-uefa-super-cup-23-24/ |access-date=16 August 2023}}</ref> City also qualified for two editions of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]]: the [[2023 FIFA Club World Cup|2023 tournament]] held in December with seven teams, and the expanded 32-team tournament in [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup|2025]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Mumford |first=Jack |url=https://www.mancity.com/news/mens/when-is-the-2023-fifa-club-world-cup-63822027 |title=When is the 2023 FIFA Club World Cup? |publisher=Manchester City F.C. |date=10 June 2023 |access-date=10 June 2023}}</ref> Following the ''Nerazzurri'' defeat, Italian teams became the first in European football history to finish runners-up in each men's [[UEFA club competitions|UEFA competition]] in a single season, including [[AS Roma|Roma]] and [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]]'s losses in the [[2023 UEFA Europa League final|Europa League final]] and in the [[2023 UEFA Europa Conference League final|Europa Conference League final]], respectively.<ref>{{cite news|language=it|first=Antonio|last=Prisco|url=https://www.ilgiornale.it/news/calcio/coppe-europee-zero-vittorie-tanti-rimpianti-calcio-italiano-2165239.html|title=Zero vittorie su tre finali europee: il flop 'record' delle italiane in coppa|work=Il Giornale|date=11 June 2023}}</ref>

